Efficient query practice

The queries below go from "works" to "works at scale." Everything is about doing more in the query and less in Apex.

Aggregate instead of counting in Apex
apex
// ❌ pulls every row into heap just to sum
Decimal total = 0;
for (Opportunity o : [SELECT Amount FROM Opportunity WHERE AccountId = :acctId]) {
    total += o.Amount;
}

// ✅ database does the math, returns one row
AggregateResult ar = [
    SELECT SUM(Amount) total FROM Opportunity WHERE AccountId = :acctId
];
Decimal total = (Decimal) ar.get('total');

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

GROUP BY for the bulk version of the same thing
apex
// one query computes the sum for EVERY account at once
Map<Id, Decimal> totalsByAccount = new Map<Id, Decimal>();
for (AggregateResult ar : [
    SELECT AccountId aid, SUM(Amount) total
    FROM Opportunity
    WHERE AccountId IN :acctIds AND StageName = 'Closed Won'
    GROUP BY AccountId
]) {
    totalsByAccount.put((Id) ar.get('aid'), (Decimal) ar.get('total'));
}

HAVING filters the aggregated groups — e.g. GROUP BY AccountId HAVING SUM(Amount) > 100000 returns only high-value accounts, without pulling the rest.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Relationship queries — one query instead of two round trips
apex
// Parent → children (subquery). Contacts arrive nested, no second query.
for (Account a : [
    SELECT Name, (SELECT Email FROM Contacts WHERE Email != null)
    FROM Account WHERE Id IN :acctIds
]) {
    for (Contact c : a.Contacts) { /* ... */ }
}

// Child → parent (dot notation). Traverse up to 5 levels.
for (Contact c : [
    SELECT LastName, Account.Name, Account.Owner.Email
    FROM Contact WHERE AccountId IN :acctIds
]) {
    String rep = c.Account.Owner.Email;
}

Child→parent traverses 5 relationships deep; parent→child subqueries go 1 level. Both replace a query-then-query-again pattern with a single call.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Selectivity — the large-data-volume killer
apex
// ❌ on a 2M-row object: Non-selective query against large object type
List<Account> old = [SELECT Id FROM Account WHERE Description != null];

// ✅ filter on an indexed field (Id, Name, external Id, audit fields, or a
//    custom field flagged External ID / Unique)
List<Account> recent = [
    SELECT Id FROM Account
    WHERE CreatedDate = LAST_N_DAYS:30 AND Type = 'Customer'
    LIMIT 10000
];

A filter is selective when it hits an index and matches under ~10% of rows (roughly 333k). Non-selective filters on a large object throw at runtime — and only in production, because your sandbox doesn't have 2M rows.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Bind the collection, don't build a string
apex
// ✅ bind a Set directly — no injection surface, no string building
Set<Id> ids = someMap.keySet();
List<Contact> cons = [SELECT Id FROM Contact WHERE AccountId IN :ids];

WHERE Id IN :aSetOfIds binds the whole collection in one query — which is exactly what makes the collect→query→map pattern possible. (And when you do need dynamic SOQL, String.escapeSingleQuotes on any user input, or Database.queryWithBinds — same injection reasoning you covered in the SOQL security days.)

*************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************

Quick reference
Instead of	                                                                                                               Do
Query in a loop	                                                                                        Collect Ids → one WHERE Id IN :set → Map
Looping to sum/count	                                                                                  SUM() / COUNT() + GROUP BY
Two queries (parent, then children)	                                                                    One subquery or dot-notation query
SELECT *-style field lists	                                                                            Only fields you read (heap)
for (x : bigListVariable)	                                                                              for (x : [SELECT...]) (streams in 200s)
Non-indexed WHERE on big objects	                                                                      Filter on indexed/selective fields + LIMIT
String-concatenated filters	                                                                            Bind variables (:collection)

A practice drill worth doing: take any query you've written, and for each one ask "what happens at 2 million rows?" If the answer involves loading them into heap or scanning a non-indexed field, that's the one to rewrite. The queries that survive that question are the efficient ones.

Given you're at Day 33-ish and just covered limits, the natural next block is asynchronous Apex — Batch, Queueable, @future, Scheduled — since that's the "use more transactions" escape hatch these fixes keep pointing at, and it's heavily tested on the PD2.

